               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="12XX" notBefore="1200" notAfter="1299" calendar="#gregorian">s. xiii</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_333">London. Dominican convent</orgName><p>16th-cent. notes in Royal 3 E.i and 3 E.ix show that 3 E.i and
                        six other vols. 'all bounde in white lether' and identifiable with 3
                        E.ii--v, vii, viii, formed a 'set' of glossed books of the greater part of
                        the bible (cf. Cat. Royal MSS., 1, p. xl). The fact that 3 E.i--v, viii
                        belonged to the London Dominicans is now recorded only on the outside of the
                        18th-cent. bindings. For 3 E.iii this is found also in Casley's
                        catalogue.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Henry Fitz-Alan, Earl of Arundel, and John,
                     Lord Lumley.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">A 16th-cent. inscr. in 3 E.i reads: 'This
                     Bible [ . . . ] a Glosse and Commentaires bounde in lether conteyne Genesis . .
                     . being in 7 volumes in folio'. The similar note in 3 E.ix (see Canterbury,
                     Franciscan convent) refers to three volumes as being 'bounde in white
                     lether'.</provenance>
               </history>
